WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.873
Graph neural networks are famously easy to fool
with small structural changes,

00:00:04.953 --> 00:00:09.325
and the field has long treated that fragility as
pure adversarial vulnerability.

00:00:09.405 --> 00:00:13.778
This ICLR 2023 paper from the Technical University
of Munich asks a sharper question:

00:00:13.858 --> 00:00:18.231
do those small perturbations actually preserve a
node's true meaning?

00:00:18.311 --> 00:00:22.684
Using a semantics-aware notion of an adversarial graph,

00:00:22.764 --> 00:00:27.136
the authors show that for low-degree nodes many
"small" edge edits already change the ground-truth label,

00:00:27.216 --> 00:00:31.589
and that every graph neural network they test is
over-robust,

00:00:31.669 --> 00:00:36.042
staying stubbornly unchanged even after the
semantics have flipped.

00:00:36.122 --> 00:00:40.495
They then show that feeding the training graph's
label structure back in through label

00:00:40.575 --> 00:00:44.947
propagation sharply cuts this over-robustness while
improving accuracy and genuine adversarial

00:00:45.027 --> 00:00:49.400
robustness.

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:56.485
An adversarial example is supposed to be a small
change that does not alter the true category of the input.

00:00:56.565 --> 00:00:59.402
For images a human can verify this by looking.

00:00:59.482 --> 00:01:02.002
For graphs there is no such visual check,

00:01:02.082 --> 00:01:06.567
so the community settled on counting edited edges
with an ℓ-zero budget.

00:01:06.647 --> 00:01:09.738
But real graphs are dominated by low-degree nodes,

00:01:09.818 --> 00:01:15.064
and for those nodes even a tiny edge budget can
completely rewire the neighbourhood.

00:01:15.144 --> 00:01:20.961
That raises a fundamental and previously unanswered
question: do these standard perturbations

00:01:21.041 --> 00:01:24.005
really keep a node's semantic content unchanged,

00:01:24.085 --> 00:01:28.000
or are we attacking nodes whose true label has
already flipped?

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:33.748
The belief that graph neural networks are easily
fooled rests on the assumption that the

00:01:33.828 --> 00:01:36.472
perturbations used are semantics-preserving.

00:01:36.552 --> 00:01:39.319
Only a few works go beyond simple edge counts,

00:01:39.399 --> 00:01:43.343
adding proxies like the degree distribution or
homophily metrics.

00:01:43.423 --> 00:01:47.924
None of them directly measure whether the
ground-truth label is preserved.

00:01:48.004 --> 00:01:53.372
This matters because if a "small" perturbation has
actually changed a node's true class,

00:01:53.452 --> 00:01:58.015
then a model that keeps its prediction fixed is not
being robustly correct,

00:01:58.095 --> 00:02:00.615
it is being wrong in a new and hidden way.

00:02:00.695 --> 00:02:03.029
The authors argue we need a principled,

00:02:03.109 --> 00:02:07.920
label-aware notion of what a semantics-preserving
graph perturbation really is.

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:11.756
The paper makes four contributions.

00:02:11.836 --> 00:02:15.292
First,

00:02:15.372 --> 00:02:18.828
it defines a semantics-aware notion of adversarial
robustness for node-level predictions,

00:02:18.908 --> 00:02:22.364
and with it a brand new concept for the graph
domain called over-robustness,

00:02:22.444 --> 00:02:25.900
which is robustness against admissible
perturbations whose ground-truth label has already changed.

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:29.436
Second,

00:02:29.516 --> 00:02:32.972
using Contextual Stochastic Block Models it shows
that common perturbation sets contain

00:02:33.052 --> 00:02:36.508
a large fraction of graphs with changed semantics
and that every examined GNN is significantly

00:02:36.588 --> 00:02:40.044
over-robust,

00:02:40.124 --> 00:02:43.580
with matching patterns on real-world graphs.

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:47.116
Third,

00:02:47.196 --> 00:02:50.652
it shows that folding the known label structure
into inference through label propagation

00:02:50.732 --> 00:02:54.188
significantly reduces over-robustness with no cost
to accuracy or adversarial robustness.

00:02:54.268 --> 00:02:57.724
Fourth,

00:02:57.804 --> 00:03:01.260
it proves that classifying an inductively sampled
node carries no robustness-accuracy tradeoff.

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:07.063
The key idea is to introduce a trusted reference
classifier that knows the true semantics.

00:03:07.143 --> 00:03:12.567
On Contextual Stochastic Block Models the authors
can derive the Bayes optimal classifier,

00:03:12.647 --> 00:03:15.686
which chooses the most likely class given the data,

00:03:15.766 --> 00:03:18.315
and use it as the ground-truth reference g.

00:03:18.395 --> 00:03:24.919
An over-robust example is then a perturbed graph
where the model and the reference agree on the clean input,

00:03:24.999 --> 00:03:29.444
the perturbation flips the reference (so the
semantics genuinely changed),

00:03:29.524 --> 00:03:32.502
but the model stubbornly keeps its old prediction.

00:03:32.582 --> 00:03:39.167
They measure over-robustness as one minus the ratio
of semantics-aware robustness to conventional robustness,

00:03:39.247 --> 00:03:45.588
so a value of zero-point-two means twenty percent
of the measured robustness lies beyond semantic change.

00:03:45.668 --> 00:03:49.685
Finally, they combine graph neural networks with
label propagation,

00:03:49.765 --> 00:03:56.350
feeding the training graph's known labels into the
inference process to reduce this unwanted over-robustness.

00:03:56.730 --> 00:04:01.081
The controlled experiments run on Contextual
Stochastic Block Models,

00:04:01.161 --> 00:04:05.319
which let the authors compute the Bayes optimal
reference exactly.

00:04:05.399 --> 00:04:09.685
Each graph has one thousand nodes and is
parametrized to mimic CORA,

00:04:09.765 --> 00:04:14.115
matching its expected number of same-class and
different-class edges.

00:04:14.195 --> 00:04:18.225
A parameter K controls how discriminative the node
features are,

00:04:18.305 --> 00:04:19.894
swept from zero-point-one,

00:04:19.974 --> 00:04:24.839
where features carry almost no signal and structure
matters most, up to five,

00:04:24.919 --> 00:04:27.150
where structure becomes unnecessary.

00:04:27.230 --> 00:04:32.544
They inductively sample a thousand test nodes per
graph and average over ten graphs.

00:04:32.624 --> 00:04:39.800
Results are corroborated on the real-world Cora-ML
graph and on a Barabási-Albert model with community structure.

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:41.799
The findings are striking.

00:04:41.879 --> 00:04:43.367
For a majority of nodes,

00:04:43.447 --> 00:04:49.051
the standard perturbation sets are full of graphs
whose true label has already changed.

00:04:49.131 --> 00:04:53.559
For example, at K equal to one and a per-node
degree-plus-two budget,

00:04:53.639 --> 00:04:59.635
ninety-nine-point-four percent of target nodes have
a perturbed graph with changed semantics.

00:04:59.715 --> 00:05:03.555
And every graph neural network tested shows
over-robustness.

00:05:03.635 --> 00:05:09.108
A perfectly robust reference like an MLP exhibits
forty-three percent over-robustness

00:05:09.188 --> 00:05:12.636
at K equal to zero-point-one under the weak ℓ₂ attack,

00:05:12.716 --> 00:05:18.517
meaning forty-three percent of its measured
adversarial robustness is actually undesirable

00:05:18.597 --> 00:05:20.738
robustness beyond semantic change,

00:05:20.818 --> 00:05:24.070
and all the GNNs cluster close to that upper bound.

00:05:24.450 --> 00:05:28.210
The most informative ablation concerns label propagation.

00:05:28.290 --> 00:05:32.050
Applying it on top of a graph neural network
sharply lowers over-robustness.

00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:35.890
For instance,

00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:39.730
a GCN combined with label propagation at K equal to
zero-point-five drops its over-robustness

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:43.570
to about twenty-one percent,

00:05:43.650 --> 00:05:47.410
and label propagation on its own achieves the
lowest over-robustness of any method.

00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:51.250
Crucially,

00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:55.090
this comes for free: adding label propagation does
not hurt test accuracy and often improves

00:05:55.170 --> 00:05:58.930
genuine adversarial robustness while structure
still matters.

00:05:59.010 --> 00:06:02.770
The effect is robust to stronger attacks too,

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:06.610
though some over-robustness always remains,

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:10.450
such as eleven-point-four percent for a GCN under
Nettack and nineteen-point-two percent for an MLP.

00:06:10.830 --> 00:06:12.989
A few numbers summarize the impact.

00:06:13.069 --> 00:06:18.681
Up to ninety-nine-point-four percent of target
nodes have perturbations that change their

00:06:18.761 --> 00:06:21.175
true label under a common threat model.

00:06:21.255 --> 00:06:27.251
A perfectly robust classifier shows forty-three
percent over-robustness at low signal strength.

00:06:27.331 --> 00:06:32.176
Label propagation cuts a GCN's over-robustness to
roughly twenty-one percent,

00:06:32.256 --> 00:06:37.293
and even under the strong Nettack attack
over-robustness never fully disappears,

00:06:37.373 --> 00:06:41.450
sitting around eleven to nineteen percent depending
on the model.

00:06:41.830 --> 00:06:46.225
The one-line takeaway is that graph neural networks
are not simply fragile,

00:06:46.305 --> 00:06:51.355
they are over-robust: a large part of their
measured robustness is stubborn robustness

00:06:51.435 --> 00:06:55.174
that persists after the node's true meaning has
already changed,

00:06:55.254 --> 00:06:58.992
which conventional evaluations wrongly credit as
good behaviour.

00:06:59.072 --> 00:07:04.123
Bringing the training graph's label structure into
inference through label propagation

00:07:04.203 --> 00:07:09.253
reduces this over-robustness while improving
accuracy and real adversarial robustness.

00:07:09.333 --> 00:07:11.521
And with a semantics-aware definition,

00:07:11.601 --> 00:07:16.174
classifying a newly added node carries no
robustness-accuracy tradeoff at all,

00:07:16.254 --> 00:07:20.410
changing how robustness in graph machine learning
ought to be measured.
